{
  "id": "eifxgf2",
  "title": "Chapter 12 – Lines, Symbols, and Views in Engineering Drawing",
  "order": 11,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "rtclldn",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Introduction"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Continuous thick lines (Type A) are used for visible outlines and edges, while continuous thin lines (Type B) are used for dimensioning, hatching, and extension lines.",
        "Hidden edges and outlines are represented by dashed thin lines, whereas center lines and lines of symmetry use chain thin lines (long-dash-dot).",
        "Orthographic projection represents a three-dimensional object through two-dimensional plane views, primarily using First Angle or Third Angle projection systems.",
        "An Isometric view is a pictorial view where three axes are inclined equally at 120 degrees to each other, maintaining true scale along isometric lines.",
        "Sectional views are used to reveal internal details and features of a part that would otherwise be obscured by hidden lines."
      ],
      "id": "gjsjrdf"
    },
